<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · date unknown · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Japji Sahib</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="pa">ਜਪੁਜੀ ਸਾਹਿਬ</bdi> / <bdi lang="pa-Latn">Japjī Sāhib</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The first prayer of the Sikh holy book, by Guru Nanak. Many Sikhs say it every morning; it asks what true worship is and what God is like.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Guru Nanak (the final Salok by Guru Angad)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (recited and chanted)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Sikhs</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Punjab region</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>the first composition of Guru Nanak, compiled by Guru Angad; date not known</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (composition of Guru Nanak, with a closing Salok by Guru Angad; printed from 1865)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a> is the composition that opens the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, the scripture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. Its original name is Jap, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> japa, the recitation of a mantra, &quot;to utter in a low voice, repeat internally&quot;; &quot;ji&quot; and &quot;Sahib&quot; were added out of respect. It is mostly the writing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> and was put together by Guru Angad: it begins with the Mool Mantra, continues with 38 paudis (stanzas) in different poetic metres, and ends with a Salok by Guru Angad. It is counted the first composition of Guru Nanak and the essence of Sikhism, which the rest of the Guru Granth Sahib expands; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> are what true worship is and what the nature of God is.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a> is the first text of the Nitnem, the daily prayers. The scholar Christopher Shackle describes it as designed for &quot;individual meditative recitation&quot; and as the first item of daily devotion for the devout. It is chanted in the morning and evening prayers of the gurdwaras, at the initiation ceremony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> and at cremations. A related text, the Jaap Sahib of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, opens the Dasam Granth; it is longer, at 199 stanzas, chiefly in Braj-Hindi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and lists some 950 names of God.</p><p>In the second half of the 19th century many printed editions (Pōthi Japji) appeared: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> from 1865, in Gurmukhi and in Perso-Arabic script, some with commentaries by Bihārī Lāl and Maṇi Siṅgh; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> in 1875 and 1882; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> in 1875 in the Khoja-Sindhi script; at Sialkot and at Rawalpindi. Ernst Trumpp printed the original text as an appendix to his translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a> (1877), with spaces between the words instead of the traditional continuous script. Translations into Hindustani appeared at Lahore in 1870 and Gujranwala in 1879, and a Hindi translation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> in 1887. Max Arthur Macauliffe included a tentative English translation in a circular letter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> dated Amritsar, 24 December 1897, and published a translation in the Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society in 1900. The Japanese scholar Tomio Mizokami later translated it into Japanese.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A meditation on the one God and on true worship: outward rites, silence or riches cannot make a person pure, which only love of the divine and remembering God's name can. It describes God as formless, without beginning or end, the author of every fortune, praised by every tradition yet beyond all words, and it closes on the value of listening, faith and grace.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>There is but one God whose name is true, the Creator, devoid of fear and enmity, immortal, unborn, self-existent; by the favour of the Guru.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Repeat His Name</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The True One was in the beginning; the True One was in the primal age.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The True One is now also, O Nanak; the True One also shall be.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>English: The opening lines, translated by Max Arthur Macauliffe, The Sikh Religion, volume 1 (1909). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Sikh_Religion%2FVolume_1%2FJapji" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
